WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:08.053
NARRATOR: Begin by framing Course Foundations
and Prerequisites as a question about inputs,

2
00:00:08.053 --> 00:00:10.515
decisions, and observable results.

3
00:00:10.515 --> 00:00:16.693
NARRATOR: The course will introduce students
into the algorithmic foundations and modern

4
00:00:16.693 --> 00:00:20.812
methods used for security evaluation and tools
design.

5
00:00:20.812 --> 00:00:31.694
NARRATOR: Consider this worked case: Students
must verify they have completed CSCI-351 or

6
00:00:31.694 --> 00:00:38.390
the combined 4003-420 and 4003-440 sequence
before enrolling.

7
00:00:38.390 --> 00:00:50.674
NARRATOR: On screen, display a prerequisite
flowchart showing CSCI-351 or the

8
00:00:50.674 --> 00:00:57.374
4003-420/4003-440 path leading to CSCI-531
enrollment.

9
00:00:57.374 --> 00:01:03.519
NARRATOR: List the givens before choosing a
method, because an unstated assumption can

10
00:01:03.519 --> 00:01:04.937
change the conclusion.

11
00:01:04.937 --> 00:01:10.687
NARRATOR: This establishes the vocabulary and
scope needed for the remaining sections.

12
00:01:10.802 --> 00:01:15.813
NARRATOR: Now define Secure Software
Development Framework Overview precisely

13
00:01:15.813 --> 00:01:20.267
enough that another learner could test the
definition.

14
00:01:20.267 --> 00:01:26.426
NARRATOR: The Secure Software Development
Framework groups practices into four practice

15
00:01:26.426 --> 00:01:26.986
groups.

16
00:01:26.986 --> 00:01:33.042
NARRATOR: Consider this worked case: Prepare
the Organization establishes people,

17
00:01:33.042 --> 00:01:39.098
processes, and technology for secure
development, while Protect the Software

18
00:01:39.098 --> 00:01:42.126
prevents unauthorized access to code.

19
00:01:42.127 --> 00:01:48.491
NARRATOR: On screen, show a four-quadrant
diagram labeling each practice group with its

20
00:01:48.491 --> 00:01:49.470
core function.

21
00:01:49.470 --> 00:01:56.220
NARRATOR: Separate the formal definition from
an implementation detail, then identify what

22
00:01:56.220 --> 00:01:58.470
evidence would distinguish them.

23
00:01:58.470 --> 00:02:05.001
NARRATOR: A precise definition makes later
examples easier to evaluate and revise.

24
00:02:05.001 --> 00:02:12.011
NARRATOR: Name each dependency explicitly, and
keep labels and representations consistent

25
00:02:12.011 --> 00:02:13.923
throughout the analysis.

26
00:02:14.042 --> 00:02:19.995
NARRATOR: With the foundation in place,
examine how Prepare the Organization Practice

27
00:02:19.995 --> 00:02:22.476
operates in a concrete sequence.

28
00:02:22.476 --> 00:02:31.227
NARRATOR: Prepare the Organization establishes
people, processes, and technology for secure

29
00:02:31.227 --> 00:02:32.023
development.

30
00:02:32.023 --> 00:02:39.092
NARRATOR: Consider this worked case: A team
creates a secure development policy and

31
00:02:39.092 --> 00:02:42.898
assigns security roles before writing code
begins.

32
00:02:42.898 --> 00:02:49.983
NARRATOR: On screen, illustrate a checklist of
people, processes, and technology setup with

33
00:02:49.983 --> 00:02:52.163
icons for each category.

34
00:02:52.163 --> 00:02:59.288
NARRATOR: Follow every arrow in order and name
the state before and after each transition.

35
00:02:59.288 --> 00:03:05.054
NARRATOR: The trace converts an abstract
statement into an inspectable sequence.

36
00:03:05.054 --> 00:03:12.162
NARRATOR: Name each dependency explicitly, and
keep labels and representations consistent

37
00:03:12.162 --> 00:03:14.101
throughout the analysis.

38
00:03:14.210 --> 00:03:21.142
NARRATOR: Next, compare the choices involved
in Protect the Software Practice and make the

39
00:03:21.142 --> 00:03:22.628
decision criteria explicit.

40
00:03:22.628 --> 00:03:28.973
NARRATOR: Protect the Software prevents
unauthorized access to code and development

41
00:03:28.973 --> 00:03:29.550
artifacts.

42
00:03:29.550 --> 00:03:35.828
NARRATOR: Consider this worked case: Source
code repositories use role-based access

43
00:03:35.828 --> 00:03:41.535
control to restrict who can view or modify
specific modules.

44
00:03:41.535 --> 00:03:49.528
NARRATOR: On screen, draw a locked vault icon
over a code repository with access control

45
00:03:49.528 --> 00:03:51.659
badges indicating restricted permissions.

46
00:03:51.659 --> 00:03:58.234
NARRATOR: Compare alternatives against the
same constraints so the evaluation does not

47
00:03:58.234 --> 00:03:59.878
move the goalposts.

48
00:03:59.878 --> 00:04:06.597
NARRATOR: Using shared criteria turns a
preference into a defensible technical choice.

49
00:04:06.597 --> 00:04:13.865
NARRATOR: Name each dependency explicitly, and
keep labels and representations consistent

50
00:04:13.865 --> 00:04:15.847
throughout the analysis.

51
00:04:15.962 --> 00:04:22.459
NARRATOR: Apply Produce Well-Secured Software
Practice to a worked situation instead of

52
00:04:22.459 --> 00:04:25.708
treating it as an isolated definition.

53
00:04:25.708 --> 00:04:31.799
NARRATOR: Produce Well-Secured Software
addresses security requirements, design,

54
00:04:31.799 --> 00:04:34.083
verification, and release.

55
00:04:34.083 --> 00:04:40.044
NARRATOR: Consider this worked case:
Developers complete a security requirements

56
00:04:40.044 --> 00:04:46.005
document and run automated verification tests
before deploying the software.

57
00:04:46.005 --> 00:04:52.489
NARRATOR: On screen, display a pipeline
diagram showing requirements, design,

58
00:04:52.489 --> 00:04:56.380
verification, and release stages with
checkmarks.

59
00:04:56.380 --> 00:05:02.673
NARRATOR: Work from the concrete inputs to the
result, checking units, types, or invariants

60
00:05:02.673 --> 00:05:04.021
at each step.

61
00:05:04.021 --> 00:05:08.708
NARRATOR: The worked case shows both how to
apply the method and how to check it.

62
00:05:08.708 --> 00:05:15.878
NARRATOR: Name each dependency explicitly, and
keep labels and representations consistent

63
00:05:15.878 --> 00:05:17.833
throughout the analysis.

64
00:05:17.954 --> 00:05:24.129
NARRATOR: Examine the tradeoffs in Respond to
Vulnerabilities Practice, including what

65
00:05:24.129 --> 00:05:27.497
improves and what becomes more difficult.

66
00:05:27.497 --> 00:05:34.024
NARRATOR: Respond to Vulnerabilities
identifies, analyzes, remediates, and learns

67
00:05:34.024 --> 00:05:36.200
from discovered weaknesses.

68
00:05:36.200 --> 00:05:42.016
NARRATOR: Consider this worked case: A
security audit finds a buffer overflow, which

69
00:05:42.016 --> 00:05:48.279
is patched, and the team updates their threat
model based on the lesson learned.

70
00:05:48.279 --> 00:05:55.972
NARRATOR: On screen, show a circular feedback
loop with arrows labeled identify, analyze,

71
00:05:55.972 --> 00:05:57.747
remediate, and learn.

72
00:05:57.747 --> 00:06:03.901
NARRATOR: State the benefit, the cost, and the
condition under which the tradeoff is

73
00:06:03.901 --> 00:06:04.341
acceptable.

74
00:06:04.341 --> 00:06:11.544
NARRATOR: Making the tradeoff visible prevents
a local improvement from hiding a larger cost.

75
00:06:11.666 --> 00:06:18.118
NARRATOR: Stress-test the reasoning around
Security Reasoning Process with an edge case

76
00:06:18.118 --> 00:06:21.881
and identify which assumption controls the
outcome.

77
00:06:21.881 --> 00:06:29.676
NARRATOR: Security reasoning identifies
assets, trust boundaries, threats, controls,

78
00:06:29.676 --> 00:06:34.006
evidence, residual risk, and responsibilities.

79
00:06:34.006 --> 00:06:40.016
NARRATOR: Consider this worked case: An
analyst maps a web application's database as

80
00:06:40.016 --> 00:06:47.412
an asset, defines the browser as a trust
boundary, and lists SQL injection as a threat.

81
00:06:47.412 --> 00:06:55.334
NARRATOR: On screen, create a flowchart
connecting assets to trust boundaries, then to

82
00:06:55.334 --> 00:06:58.990
threats, controls, and residual risk
calculations.

83
00:06:58.990 --> 00:07:05.182
NARRATOR: Change one assumption at a time and
observe which part of the reasoning no longer

84
00:07:05.182 --> 00:07:05.569
holds.

85
00:07:05.569 --> 00:07:11.899
NARRATOR: The edge case reveals the boundary
of the claim without discarding the useful

86
00:07:11.899 --> 00:07:12.803
core idea.

87
00:07:12.914 --> 00:07:20.058
NARRATOR: Use Cryptographic Standards and
Algorithms to diagnose a failure by separating

88
00:07:20.058 --> 00:07:23.035
symptoms, causes, and corrective action.

89
00:07:23.035 --> 00:07:29.160
NARRATOR: Encryption supports confidentiality
when keys and modes are used correctly.

90
00:07:29.160 --> 00:07:36.293
NARRATOR: Consider this worked case: AES-256
is an approved algorithm that provides

91
00:07:36.293 --> 00:07:42.832
confidentiality for data at rest when
implemented with proper key management.

92
00:07:42.832 --> 00:07:49.648
NARRATOR: On screen, list approved algorithms
like AES and RSA with their corresponding

93
00:07:49.648 --> 00:07:52.270
security parameters and use cases.

94
00:07:52.270 --> 00:07:58.056
NARRATOR: Locate the first point where
observed behavior diverges from expected

95
00:07:58.056 --> 00:08:01.738
behavior, then test the smallest plausible
cause.

96
00:08:01.738 --> 00:08:07.738
NARRATOR: The diagnosis is strongest when
another person can reproduce the observation

97
00:08:07.738 --> 00:08:09.238
and the test.

98
00:08:09.362 --> 00:08:15.485
NARRATOR: Connect Modes of Operation and Key
Management to the earlier ideas and explain

99
00:08:15.485 --> 00:08:18.546
how the pieces work as one system.

100
00:08:18.546 --> 00:08:24.915
NARRATOR: Modes of operation define how a
primitive processes data larger than one

101
00:08:24.915 --> 00:08:25.405
block.

102
00:08:25.405 --> 00:08:32.402
NARRATOR: Consider this worked case: CBC mode
processes multi-block data by chaining

103
00:08:32.402 --> 00:08:39.983
ciphertext blocks, and key management ensures
keys are rotated regularly to limit exposure.

104
00:08:39.983 --> 00:08:47.432
NARRATOR: On screen, illustrate a block cipher
processing multiple data blocks sequentially,

105
00:08:47.432 --> 00:08:53.640
alongside a key lifecycle diagram showing
rotation and revocation steps.

106
00:08:53.640 --> 00:09:02.690
NARRATOR: Explain each connection explicitly
and verify that information retains the same

107
00:09:02.690 --> 00:09:04.952
meaning across representations.

108
00:09:04.952 --> 00:09:11.945
NARRATOR: The synthesis matters only if every
connection remains supported by the original

109
00:09:11.945 --> 00:09:12.483
evidence.

110
00:09:12.602 --> 00:09:18.360
NARRATOR: Finish with Security Evaluation and
Practical Application as a practical checklist

111
00:09:18.360 --> 00:09:21.239
that can guide a new analysis.

112
00:09:21.239 --> 00:09:26.904
NARRATOR: Security depends on the complete
protocol and implementation, not only an

113
00:09:26.904 --> 00:09:27.848
algorithm name.

114
00:09:27.848 --> 00:09:35.216
NARRATOR: Consider this worked case: A system
using a standard encryption algorithm fails

115
00:09:35.216 --> 00:09:40.317
security evaluation because the implementation
has a side-channel vulnerability.

116
00:09:40.317 --> 00:09:47.778
NARRATOR: On screen, show a comparison chart
between algorithm name vs. implementation

117
00:09:47.778 --> 00:09:52.130
security, highlighting side-channel risks and
validation checkpoints.

118
00:09:52.130 --> 00:09:59.709
NARRATOR: For each checklist item, record the
evidence, the remaining uncertainty, and the

119
00:09:59.709 --> 00:10:01.458
next verification step.

120
00:10:01.458 --> 00:10:07.361
NARRATOR: The result is a repeatable
procedure, not a conclusion that depends on

121
00:10:07.361 --> 00:10:08.723
memory or intuition.
